[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from January 19, 2019,
[00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.360]   titled An Update on the SaaS Rule of 40.
[00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:12.160]   Thanks to the folks at Piper Jaffray and their recently published 2018 software market review,
[00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.880]   we can take a look at a recent chart that plots public software company enterprise value,
[00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.040]   EVV versus Rule of 40, Rule of 40, score free cash flow margin.
[00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:27.120]   Press revenue growth rate. As a reminder, the Rule of 40 is an industry rule of thumb that says
[00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.600]   a high growth SaaS company can burn as much cash as it likes in order to drive growth,
[00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.240]   as long as its growth rate is 40 percentage points higher than its free cash flow margin.
[00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.400]   It's an attempt at devising a simple rule to help software companies with the complex question of
[00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.800]   how to balance growth and profitability. One past study showed that while Rule of
[00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.880]   40 compliant software companies made up a little more than half of all public software companies
[00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.640]   that they captured more than half of all public software companies that they captured more than
[00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.920]   80% of all public market cap. Let's take a look at Piper's chart which plots Rule of 40 score on
[00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:04.000]   the x-axis and enterprise value, EV, divided by revenue on the y-axis. It also plots a presumably
[00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.840]   least squares fit line through the data points. Source, PJC analysis, and SAP Capital IQ as of
[00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:16.080]   December 31, 2018. Of note, less than half of all companies in this set are Rule of 40 compliant.
[00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.560]   The median Rule of 40 score was 31.7%. The median multiple for companies in the set was 6.6 times.
[00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.360]   The slope of the line is 12, meaning that for each 10 percentage points of Rule of 40 score
[00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.520]   improvement, a company's revenue multiple increases by 1.2 times. R to the 2 is 0.42,
[00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.080]   which, if I recall correctly, means that the Rule of 40 score explains 42% of the
[00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.200]   variability of the data. So, while there's lots it doesn't explain, it's still a useful indicator.
[00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.880]   A few nerdier things of note. Remember that the line is only valid in the data range presented.
[00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.680]   Since no companies had a negative Rule of 40 score, it would be invalid extrapolation to simply
[00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.120]   continue the line down and to the left. Early-stage startup executives often misapply these charts
[00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.000]   for getting the selection bias within them. Every company on the chart did well enough at some point
[00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.520]   in terms of size and growth to become a public SaaS company. Just because live person LPSN has
[00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.800]   a 4 times multiple within Rule of 40 score of 10% doesn't mean your $20 million startup with
[00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.120]   the same score is also worth 4 times. As, LPSN is a much bigger company, roughly $250 million,
[00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.320]   and already cleared many hurdles to get there. The big question around the Rule of 40 is,
[00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.440]   when should companies start to target it? A superstar like Elastic had 76% growth and 8%
[00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:38.560]   FCF margin, so a Rule of 40 score of 84% at its spectacular IPO. However, Avalara had 26% growth
[00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.840]   and 28% FCF margin for a Rule of 40 score of 2% and its IPO went fine. Ditto Annapalan. I'll be
[00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.160]   doing some work in the next few months to try and get better data on Rule of 40 trajectory into an
[00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.760]   IPO. My instinct at this point is that many companies target Rule of 40 compliance too early,
[00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.760]   sacrifice growth in a process, and hurt their valuations because they fail to deliver high
[00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.200]   growth and don't get the assumed customer acquisition cost efficiencies built in the
[00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.080]   financial models, which end up, as one friend called them, spreadsheet-induced hallucinations.
[00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.480]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
