[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.920]   While the CPP formula outputs a long 33.3-month CAC payback period, reality is far, far worse.
[00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.780]   One problem with the CPP formula is that it does not factor in churn and how exposed a
[00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.440]   cohort is to it. The more chances customers have to not renew during the payback period,
[00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.800]   the more you need to consider the possibility of non-renewal in your math. In this example,
[00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.480]   when you properly account for churn, you still have $6 worth of CAC to recover after 30 years.
[00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.920]   You literally never get back your CAC to recover after 30 years.
