[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000]   Here we go again. Say the CAC payback period formula outputs 33 months. Is the real CPP 33
[00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.600]   months or one day? Same argument, it's one day. But the formula outputs 33 months? Yes,
[00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.760]   but the CAC recovery time is one day. If you want to look at something else, then pick another
[00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.240]   metric. It gets even harder. Now imagine a company that does half one-year deals and half three-year
[00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.320]   deals and half three-year deals on an ARR-weighted basis. Let's assume it has a CAC ratio of 1.5,
[00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.200]   75% subscription gross margins, and thus a notional CAC payback period of 24 months.
[00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.760]   Let's see what really happens using a model.
