[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from March 17, 2016, titled "CAC Payback Period,
[00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.360]   The Most Misunderstood SAS Metric." Note, this post includes images. See the original post on
[00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.120]   Kellogg for visuals. The single most misunderstood software as a service SAS metric I've encountered
[00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.400]   is the CAC Payback Period, CPP, a compound metric that is generally defined as the months
[00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.680]   of contribution margin to pay back the cost of acquiring a customer. Bessemer defines the CPP
[00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.000]   as "I quibble with some of the bessemerisms in the definition." For example, one, most enterprise
[00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.480]   SAS companies should use annual recurring revenue (ARR), not monthly recurring revenue (MRR) because
[00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.320]   most enterprise companies are doing annual, not monthly, contracts. Two, the committed, MRR concept
[00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.880]   is an overreach because it includes anticipated churn which is basically impossible to measure
[00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.800]   and often unknown. And three, I don't know why they use the prior period for both S&M costs and
[00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:06.400]   new ARR. Almost everybody else uses prior period S&M divided by current period. ARR in customer A,
[00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.680]   acquisition costs CAC calculations on the theory that last quarter's S&M generated this quarter's
[00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:18.320]   new ARR. Switching to ARR nomenclature, and with a quick slight of mathematical hand for simplification,
[00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.680]   I define the CAC Payback Period, CPP, as follows. Let's run some numbers. If your company has a CAC
[00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.400]   ratio of 1.5 and subscription gross margins of 75%, then your CPP 24 months. If your company has
[00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:39.520]   a CAC ratio of 1.2 and subscription gross margins of 80%, then your CPP 18 months. If your company
[00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.160]   has a CAC ratio of 0.8 and subscription gross margins at 80%, then your CPP 12 months. All
[00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.520]   seems pretty simple, right? Not so fast. There are two things that constantly confound people
[00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.360]   when looking at CAC Payback Period's CPPU. They forget payback metrics are risk metrics, not return
[00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.400]   metrics. They fail to correctly interpret the impact of annual or multi-year contracts. Payback
[00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.280]   metrics offer risk, not return quick. Basic MBA. Question, you have two projects. Both require an
[00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.840]   investment of 100 units, and you have only 100 units to invest. Which do you pick? Project A,
[00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.480]   which has a payback period of 12 months. Project B, which has a payback period of 6 months quick.
[00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.160]   Which do you pick? Well, Project B, duh. But do you pick? Well, Project B, duh.
[00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:29.200]   Project A has a net present value NPV of 500 units. Project B has an NPV of 110 units.
[00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.840]   Well, don't you feel silly for picking Project B? Payback is all about how long your money is
[00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.840]   committed so it can't be used for other projects, and at risk meaning you might not get it back.
[00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.760]   Payback doesn't tell you anything about return. In capital budgeting, NPV tells you about return.
[00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.160]   In a SaaS business, customer lifetime value, LTV, tells you about return.
[00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.720]   There are situations where it makes a lot of sense to look at CPP. For example, if you're running a
[00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.320]   monthly SaaS service with a high churn rate then you need to look closely how long you're putting
[00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.640]   your money at risk because there is a very real chance you won't recoup your CAC investment,
[00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.760]   let alone get any return on it. Consider a monthly SaaS company with a $3,500 customer
[00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:12.720]   acquisition cost, subscription gross margin of 70%, a monthly fee of $100, and 3% monthly churn.
[00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.000]   I'll calculate the ratios and examine the churn. I'll calculate the ratios and examine the CAC
[00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.560]   recovery of 100 customer cohort. While the CPP formula outputs a long 33.3-month CAC payback
[00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:30.320]   period, reality is far, far worse. One problem with the CPP formula is that it does not factor
[00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.040]   in churn and how exposed a cohort is to it. The more chances customers have to not renew during
[00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.600]   the payback period, the more you need to consider the possibility of non-renewal in your math.
[00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.720]   In this example, when you properly account for churn, you still have $6 worth of CAC to recover
[00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.880]   after 30 years. You literally never get back your CAC to recover after 30 years. Soapbox,
[00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.720]   this is another case where using a model is infinitely preferable to back-of-the-envelope
[00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.840]   BOTT analysis using SaaS metrics. If you want to understand the financials of a SaaS company,
[00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.560]   then build a driver-based model and vary the drivers. In this case and many others,
[00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.440]   BOTT analysis fails due to subtle complexity, whereas a well-built model will always produce
[00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.640]   correct answers, even if they are counterintuitive. Such cases aside, the real problem with being
[00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.160]   too focused on CAC payback period is that CPP is a risk metric that tells you nothing about returns.
[00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.320]   Companies are in business to get returns, not simply to minimize risk,
[00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.120]   so to properly analyze a SaaS business we need to look at both.
[00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.360]   The impact of annual and multi-year prepaid contracts on CAC payback period. The CPP formula
[00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.680]   outputs a payback period in months, but most enterprise SaaS businesses today run on an
[00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.800]   annual rhythm. Despite pricing that is sometimes still stated per user per month, SaaS companies
[00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.800]   realized years ago that enterprise customers preferred annual contracts and actually disliked
[00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.480]   monthly invoicing. Just as MRR is a bit of a relic from the old SaaS days, so is a CAC payback
[00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:59.200]   period stated in months. In a 100% annual prepaid contract world, the CPP formula should output in
[00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:05.520]   multiples of 12, rounding up for all values greater than 12. For example, if a company's CAC payback
