[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from September 1, 2015 titled, "I Don't
[00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.380]   Want to Talk to You Anymore."
[00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.400]   One time, back in the day at Business Objects, we were all flying back from Paris to San
[00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.600]   Francisco, when the plane pulled ten feet back from the gate and then stopped.
[00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.600]   The pilot announced that we were taking a delay of several hours.
[00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.620]   Frustrated, one of our board members, a very polished, powerful, statuesque man, immediately
[00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.160]   asked the flight attendant if he could get off the plane.
[00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.940]   He wanted to take another flight and felt unfairly trapped.
[00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.940]   She said no.
[00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.800]   A polite dispute ensued.
[00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.560]   As we, the management team, watched in awe of his calm, yet firm, argumentative style,
[00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.900]   a strange thing happened.
[00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.000]   "I don't want to talk to you any more," he said.
[00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.440]   "I want to speak to the pilot."
[00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.520]   "But, but, but, what do you mean you need to talk to me, because, uh, uh, uh, uh, I
[00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.680]   don't want to talk to you any more," he repeated.
[00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.200]   For years, many of us on the executive team would joke about how one day our terminations
[00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.200]   might go down.
[00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.080]   But you don't understand, the seminar attendance was low because there was a blizzard that closed
[00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.680]   the roads and shut down public transportation.
[00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.280]   All I know is we failed to achieve our lead generation goal.
[00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.840]   But it was a freak April snowstorm.
[00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.340]   "I don't want to talk to you any more," "but, but, uh, uh, I don't want to talk
[00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.480]   to you any more," the phrase developed a certain legend status to it.
[00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.360]   "I'd forgotten about it for years until one day at Mark Logic.
[00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.440]   I was supposed to meet with one of my direct reports, and I didn't want to.
[00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.680]   I wasn't looking forward to it.
[00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.640]   I didn't want to talk to the person any more."
[00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.160]   And then a huge cut check went off.
[00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.480]   "Wait a minute.
[00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.640]   What does it mean when I don't want to talk to the person who runs at my company is an
[00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.120]   important part of the company.
[00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.320]   I run the company.
[00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.560]   I am hugely committed to the company's success, which cannot happen without success in.
[00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.560]   How can this be?
[00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.060]   In business, we are generally taught to be logical and data-driven, which lines up very
[00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.880]   well with my natural style.
[00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.040]   But this was emotional.
[00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.040]   This was a feeling.
[00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.240]   I didn't want to talk with someone.
[00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.240]   What did it mean?
[00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.880]   Should I listen to the feeling or ignore it?
[00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.360]   I didn't want to talk with someone.
[00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.360]   What did it mean?
[00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.040]   Should I listen to the feeling or ignore it?"
[00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.120]   It got me to thinking about why I wouldn't want to meet with someone.
[00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.600]   Generically, why would I not want to speak to one of my direct reports?
[00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.240]   I started to generate classes of people who I wouldn't want to talk to.
[00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.240]   People who don't listen.
[00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.800]   There's no point in talking to someone who doesn't listen.
[00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.620]   It gets boring over time.
[00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.100]   People who don't follow through.
[00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.360]   What good is agreeing to a plan and then have it not get executed?
[00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.640]   People who can't keep up.
[00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.080]   When someone is over their head in a job, they can't keep up with the conversation.
[00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.520]   Who wants to talk to someone when you have to keep backing up and slowing down?
[00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.520]   People who griff.
[00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.520]   KU.
[00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.660]   Who wants to talk to people who nod their head in agreement when you know they disagree?
[00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.440]   People who can't or won't change?
[00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.940]   How many times do you want to have the same converse?
[00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:28.940]   A.I.
[00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:29.940]   N?
[00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.940]   People who are negative.
[00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:34.640]   A huge amount of business is identifying and solving problems, but it can always be done
[00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.360]   a positive constructive way.
[00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.500]   Who wants to talk to Debbie Downer every day?
[00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.500]   People who are mean.
[00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.820]   There's a reason.
[00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.160]   The No Asshole rule is one of my favorite books, and it's not just because I think
[00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.760]   the world of Bob Sutton.
[00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.500]   Once I generated this list, I began to realize that the feeling was hugely important.
[00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.440]   If I didn't want to meet with one of my direct reports, if I didn't want to talk to
[00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.800]   them anymore, it was no small sign, it was no small sign, it was a indicator of a potentially
[00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.100]   huge problem.
[00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.120]   So now I listen to the feeling.
[00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.280]   Because I now know that if I don't want to talk to you anymore, then it's a sign that
[00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.360]   someone is in one of the above classes and that's an issue we need to, well, talk about.
[00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.280]   Whether I want to or not.
[00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.960]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and A.I. with permission from Dave.
