[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540]   Think, I know the formula you're using says 18 months, but I'm holding an invoice, or
[00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.240]   if you wait 30 days, check in my hand for more than the customer acquisition cost.
[00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.680]   Or remember from B-School that payback periods are supposed to measure risk, no return, and
[00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.980]   to do so by measuring how long your money is on the table.
[00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.480]   Or the problem with your formula is you're producing a continuous result in a world where
[00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.120]   you actually only collect modulo 12 months.
[00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.840]   Isn't that a problem for a would-be payback metric?
