[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from January 30th, 2019, titled "Rule of 40
[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.960]   Glideslope Planning." Enterprise SaaS companies need a lot of money to grow. The median company
[00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:18.160]   spends $1.32 to acquire $1.0 in annual recurring revenue (ARR). They need to make that investment
[00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.160]   for 14 years before getting to an IPO. It all adds up to a median of $300 million in capital
[00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.920]   raised prior to an IPO. With such fast amounts of money in play, some say it's a growth at all costs.
[00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.960]   But others hold to the rule of 40, which attempts to balance growth and profitability with a simple
[00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.280]   rule. Grow as fast as you want as long as your revenue growth rate plus your free cash flow
[00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.160]   margin, 40%. The rule of 40 gets a lot of attention, but I think that companies are not
[00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.400]   asking the right question about it. The right question is not "when should my growing startup
[00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.200]   be rule of 40 compliant?" For more than half of all public SaaS companies, the answer to that
[00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.240]   question, by the way, is "not yet." For multiple studies, I've read the median rule of 40 score
[00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:03.360]   for public SaaS companies is E31%, meaning that more than half of public SaaS companies are not
[00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.080]   rule of 40 compliant. So, unless you're an absolutely amazing company like Elastic,
[00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.600]   which had a rule of 40 score of 87% at its IPO, you probably shouldn't be unrealistically planning
[00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.720]   to become rule of 40 compliant three years before your IPO. If you do, especially if you're well
[00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.040]   funded and don't need additional expense constraints, you might well compromise growth with a premature
[00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.640]   focus on the rule of 40, which could shoot off your corporate foot in terms of your eventual
[00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.760]   valuation. If "when should we be rule of 40 compliant?" is the wrong question then. "When
[00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.320]   should we be rule of 40 compliant?" is the wrong question. "What should my company's rule of 40
[00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.880]   glide slope be?" That is, over the next several years, what is your eventual rule of 40 score
[00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.200]   target and how do you want to evolve to it? The big advantage of this question is that the answer
[00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.600]   isn't a year, and it doesn't assume rule of 40 compliance, but it does get you to start thinking
[00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.320]   about and tracking your rule of 40 score. I built a little model to help do some what-if analysis
[00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.800]   around this question. You can download it here. In our example, we've got a five-year-old,
[00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.680]   thirty million dollars ARR SAS company planning the next five years of its evolution, hopefully
