[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from Mather 2026 titled "The Re-emergence of
[00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.240]   Product Line Marketing." A few years ago I wrote a post called "The Decomposition of Marketing."
[00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.240]   The argument was that startup CEOs were increasingly breaking marketing apart,
[00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.120]   moving product marketing under product and moving demand gen under sales, in order to get strong
[00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.680]   leadership in both product marketing and demand gen without having to find the rare CMO who's
[00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.640]   genuinely great at both. I think that trend has accelerated, and it's created a problem that
[00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.440]   nobody seems to be talking about. Mind the gap. Here's what happens when you decompose marketing
[00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.040]   in a multi-product organization. In the fully decomposed organization, there is no traditional
[00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.280]   CMO. What you have instead is three distinct leaders, an SVP of demand gen who reports to
[00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.040]   the CRO and is laser focused on pipeline, one or more VPs of product marketing who report into the
[00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.120]   CPO or/or product GMs and own their individual products, and an SVP of corporate marketing,
[00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.840]   sometimes carrying the CMO title, sometimes not, who owns brand and communications.
[00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.480]   Each product team has its own product marketer, or team leader, focused on their product's
[00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.200]   positioning, messaging, and launch. The CPO loves it because they no longer have to work,
[00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.920]   that is, waste time with marketing to build their story. The product marketers love it because
[00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.000]   they're not spread thin. The demand gen leader even likes it because they know who to work with
[00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.360]   on product-specific campaigns. The problem is that you now have three product marketers,
[00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.720]   each telling a story of their individual product. Each one is perfectly capable of answering "why
[00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.800]   buy mine" for their specific product. None of them is positioned, organizationally or politically,
