[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from May 21, 2019 titled "The Rule of 40, Down
[00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.560]   but Not Out." Neeraj Agrawal and Logan Bartlett of Battery Ventures recently published the 2019
[00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.280]   version of its Outstanding Annual Software Roundup report. I highly recommend this report,
[00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.720]   it's 78 pages chock full of great data about topics like "Why Battery is Long Software Overall,"
[00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.080]   "The Four Errors of Software Evolution," "The Five Forces Driving Software's Accelerating Growth,"
[00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.520]   key trends in 2018, including setting records in three areas, one public company revenue multiples,
[00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.840]   two IPO volume by over two times, and three M&A volume by over two times. Key trends from their
[00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.280]   2018 report that are still alive, well, and driving software businesses. But, most of all,
[00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.640]   it has some great charts on the Rule of 40 that I want to present and discuss here.
[00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.280]   Before doing that, I must note that I drank today's morning coffee reading Alex Clayton's.
[00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.960]   Reiki PO Breakdown, a great post about a cloud security company with absolutely stunning growth
[00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:08.800]   at scale, 21% growth to $3-12 million in ending ARR and FY19, and, despite my headline, well in
[00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.080]   compliance with the Rule of 40, 11-10% revenue growth, plus 2-6% free cash flow margin, 84%,
[00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.520]   one of the highest Rule of 40 scores that I've ever seen. Keep an eye on this company, I expect it
[00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:27.360]   should have a strong IPO. However, finding one superstar neither proves nor disproves the rule.
[00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.080]   Let's turn to the battery data to do that. When discussing the Rule of 40, most financial analysts
[00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.280]   make one of two plots. They do a scatterplot with revenue growth on the X-axis and FCF margin on the
[00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.680]   Y-axis. The Rule of 40 then becomes a line that separates the chart into two zones,
[00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.800]   compliant and non-compliant. Note that a minority of public companies actually comply suggesting the
[00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.000]   Rule of 40 is a pretty high bar, or more interestingly, they do a linear regression of Rule of 40 score
[00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.320]   versus enterprise, value/revenue EV multiple. This puts focus on the question, what's the relationship
[00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.640]   between Rule of 40 score and company value? But that thing has always bugged me is that
[00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.920]   nobody does the linear regress. Eye on against both the Rule of 40 score and revenue growth.
[00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.360]   Nobody, until battery. Here's what it shows. First, let's look at the classic Rule of 40
[00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.000]   regression. Recall that R-squared is a statistical measure that explains the dependence of the
[00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.600]   dependent variable, in this case, EV multiple, on the independent variable, Rule of 40 score.
[00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.560]   Here you can see that about 58% of the variation in enterprise value multiple is explained by
[00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.080]   Rule of 40 score. You can intuit that by looking at the dots relative to the line. While there is
[00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.720]   clearly some linear correlation between the data, it's a long way from perfect, that is, lots of
[00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.000]   dots are pretty far from the line. Now the fun part. Let's see the same regression against revenue
[00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.320]   growth alone. R-squared here is 51%. So the explanatory power of the Rule of 40 is only 7%
[00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.800]   higher than revenue growth alone. Probably still worth looking at, but it sure gets a lot of PR
[00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.240]   for explaining only an incremental 7%. It could be worse, I suppose. Rule of 40 could have a lower
[00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.920]   R-squared than revenue growth alone. In fact, it did back in 2008 and in 2012. In the vein,
[00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.280]   for some real fun, let's look at how this relationship has changed over time.
[00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.720]   The first thing you'll notice is that pre-2012, both last 12-month, LTM revenue growth and the
